diff --git a/doc/getting-started.rst b/doc/getting-started.rst
index 35bd388fa9b8..c2c3e86d34c9 100644
--- a/doc/getting-started.rst
+++ b/doc/getting-started.rst
@@ -447,6 +447,32 @@ Or tell umpf to rebase onto a new *umpf-base* when creating a fresh *utag*::
     # umpf-topic-range: 8bae5bbec8cb4599c141405e9755b7c0e42e064f..19cdc2b857e662a38c712b41ce610000a5ddc6ae
     # umpf-end
 
+Comparing *utags*
+-----------------
+
+After rebasing onto a new *umpf-base*, a plain ``git diff`` between the old
+and the new *utag* contains all upstream changes as well. To only review
+how the patch stack itself changed, use ``umpf range-diff``::
+
+   ~/linux $  umpf range-diff 5.0/special-customer-release/20190311-1 5.0/special-customer-release/20230309-2
+
+It looks up the *umpf-base* and the linear patch stack of both *utags* and
+runs ``git range-diff`` on them. Instead of a *utag*, a qualified *useries*
+file can be given, e.g. the ``series`` file written by ``umpf format-patch``.
+If the second *umpf* is omitted, ``HEAD`` is used, which is convenient right
+after ``umpf tag``.
+
+Options after ``--`` are passed to ``git range-diff``, e.g. to show only the
+list of changed, added and dropped patches::
+
+   ~/linux $  umpf range-diff 5.0/special-customer-release/20190311-1 -- --no-patch
+
+The release commits of both *utags* are tiny, but differ in the version and
+often in the context after a rebase. So that ``git range-diff`` still pairs
+them instead of showing a dropped and an added commit, umpf passes
+``--creation-factor=100``; another ``--creation-factor`` after ``--`` overrides
+it.
+
 Synchronizing umpf topic branch
 -------------------------------
 
